    {
      "id": "moment-ffce77d3-1fb1-4f90-8d57-6da5c47b1beb",
      "experienceId": "experience-costa-rica-2025-rancho-la-merced",
      "postId": "ffce77d3-1fb1-4f90-8d57-6da5c47b1beb",
      "title": "Uvita Horses",
      "notes": "I’m riding through the forest at Rancho La Merced in Uvita, with monkeys overhead and a small group of fellow riders around me. There are maybe ten of us, visiting during shoulder season. I often look for animal experiences when I travel, and horses tend to be part of that. This ride takes us through the ranch, across a few rivers and down to the beach, roughly three hours from saddle up to saddle down.\n\nOn the beach, our guide lets those of us comfortable in the saddle pick up the pace. Those horses can move. There’s the sound of the ocean, the galloping in the sand and the horses’ grunts. Seeing monkeys from horseback is already something, but this stretch of the ride is hard to beat.",
      "travelDate": "06/02/2025",
      "sortOrder": 0,
      "visibility": "published",
      "mediaAssetIds": [
        "video-costa-rica-2025-2568e61dfd18-001"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "3f884f845a9081a8ff558268214e47931f703a1be2eaf99f52f7b7d88ea638de"
    },
    {
      "id": "moment-fb822fa8-db21-4f7d-abcd-6c7e1ab36cc2",
      "experienceId": "experience-costa-rica-2025-uvita-the-lost-lemon",
      "postId": "fb822fa8-db21-4f7d-abcd-6c7e1ab36cc2",
      "title": "Uvita House",
      "notes": "Our Airbnb in Uvita feels secluded without putting us far from everything. The shoulder season brings a mix of good weather and rain, and I’m glad we have a private pool for time in the water. I also appreciate something less glamorous, laundry on the property. Between hikes and traveling, we haven’t had much time to catch up on it.\n\nA covered, raised, open air yoga studio sits just a few steps from the back door. We opt for a massage session organized by our host, and it’s well worth it. The pool is a great part of staying here, but the practical comforts count too.",
      "travelDate": "06/02/2025",
      "sortOrder": 0,
      "visibility": "published",
      "mediaAssetIds": [
        "video-costa-rica-2025-881770ae4854-001"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "2bb0c27170452d327817feb9d2be35301e1d63530c30737906984402af3d943b"
    },
    {
      "id": "moment-73b2e53d-cb2f-4b81-8137-0f02b6f51e20",
      "experienceId": "experience-costa-rica-2025-uvita",
      "postId": "73b2e53d-cb2f-4b81-8137-0f02b6f51e20",
      "title": "High Tide Whale Tail",
      "notes": "The water reaches almost to the tree line at Uvita’s Whale Tail. We arrive at high tide, with barely any beach left and the park nearly empty. After the past couple of days driving and trekking, I’m ready for an easier day. We’ve packed snacks, water and swim gear, but there isn’t much room to settle in.\n\nInstead, we wander the narrow stretch of coast and make a local friend. A dog hangs out with us for a good hour as we explore what the tide has left exposed. I keep noticing how little land remains between the water and the trees.\n\nWe leave with a plan to return at low tide, when we can walk out to the Whale Tail. For now, it’s a coastal wander with a dog for company.",
      "travelDate": "06/02/2025",
      "sortOrder": 0,
      "visibility": "published",
